    {
      "id": "jOmbEo73NlqTVpw6v8Hp1",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -124.90340818141351,
      "y": -436.39206168927,
      "width": 915.0394897460938,
      "height": 925,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a7",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 4397826,
      "version": 592,
      "versionNonce": 174460994,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1787558156626,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. class - A blueprint\n2. Object - A instance of class\n3. Enum - fixed set of named constants, type-safed\n4. Access Modifiers - \n    - (+) public - can access anywhere in app - send_email()\n    - (-) private - only inside the same class - __log_change()\n    - (#) protected - same package and child classess - _is_valid_email()\n5. Interface - it defines a contract. It tells a class must do without deciding\n    how it should do it.\n    - Use an interface when you want to define a contract\n    - from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n      class FileStorage(ABC):\n        @abstractmethod\n        def save(self, file_name, data):\n            pass\n6. Abstract class - A class can't be instanced directly. It can provide shared fields \n    and methods while leaving some behavior for child classes to implement.\n    - Use an abstract class when closely related classes need to share common code or state.\n    --  import json\n        from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n        \n        class ReportGenerator(ABC):\n            def generate(self, data):\n                content = self.format(data)\n                self.save(content)\n        \n            def save(self, content):\n                print(f\"Saving report: {content}\")\n        \n            @abstractmethod\n            def format(self, data):\n                pass\n        \n        class CsvReportGenerator(ReportGenerator):\n            def format(self, data):\n                return \",\".join(data)\n    ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. class - A blueprint\n2. Object - A instance of class\n3. Enum - fixed set of named constants, type-safed\n4. Access Modifiers - \n    - (+) public - can access anywhere in app - send_email()\n    - (-) private - only inside the same class - __log_change()\n    - (#) protected - same package and child classess - _is_valid_email()\n5. Interface - it defines a contract. It tells a class must do without deciding\n    how it should do it.\n    - Use an interface when you want to define a contract\n    - from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n      class FileStorage(ABC):\n        @abstractmethod\n        def save(self, file_name, data):\n            pass\n6. Abstract class - A class can't be instanced directly. It can provide shared fields \n    and methods while leaving some behavior for child classes to implement.\n    - Use an abstract class when closely related classes need to share common code or state.\n    --  import json\n        from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n        \n        class ReportGenerator(ABC):\n            def generate(self, data):\n                content = self.format(data)\n                self.save(content)\n        \n            def save(self, content):\n                print(f\"Saving report: {content}\")\n        \n            @abstractmethod\n            def format(self, data):\n                pass\n        \n        class CsvReportGenerator(ReportGenerator):\n            def format(self, data):\n                return \",\".join(data)\n    ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
