    {
      "id": "GBWmAKtYta8S_EZGcc9CY",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 870.6771390769338,
      "y": -454.36083949723024,
      "width": 1006.3192749023438,
      "height": 800,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aB",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1513330910,
      "version": 1522,
      "versionNonce": 619331870,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1787558156626,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Encapsulation(data together):\n    - Keeping data attributes, methods into class, how data can modify/access from outside.\n    - it protects objects from invalid states and keep the logic of managing data in one-place.\n    - Access modifiers will help in encapsulation\n        -->  class BankAccount:\n            def __init__(self):\n                self.__balance = 0  # \"private\" via name mangling\n\n2. Abstraction(wall):\n    - Hide the unnecessary internal details to outside\n    - Interfaces and abstract classes are two common ways to create abstractions.\n\n3. Inheritance(parent-child):\n    - allows one class to reuse , extends fields,methods to another class(child)\n    - It represents \"is-a\" relationship.\n    -- > class EmailNotification(Notification):\n            def send(self, message):\n                print(f\"Sending email to {self.recipient}: {message}\")\na. Composition(param-class):\n    - Instead of inheriting behavior, simply pass the object into class as params\n    - creates loosser coupling bcz dependency can be replaced without changing the class hierarchy\n        --> class OrderService:\n            def __init__(self, email_sender):\n                self.email_sender = email_sender\n4. Polymorphism:\n    - same method name/interfaces to exhibit different behaviors depending on objects\n    - Compile-time Polymorphism(Method Overloading):\n        - methods with same name , but different params.\n        - python NOT support for add(int,int,int) -- use default - def add(self, *args): return sum(args)\n    - Runtime Polymorphism(Method Overriding):\n        - child class provides new implemenation for parent's method with same name,params\n            but different behavior.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Encapsulation(data together):\n    - Keeping data attributes, methods into class, how data can modify/access from outside.\n    - it protects objects from invalid states and keep the logic of managing data in one-place.\n    - Access modifiers will help in encapsulation\n        -->  class BankAccount:\n            def __init__(self):\n                self.__balance = 0  # \"private\" via name mangling\n\n2. Abstraction(wall):\n    - Hide the unnecessary internal details to outside\n    - Interfaces and abstract classes are two common ways to create abstractions.\n\n3. Inheritance(parent-child):\n    - allows one class to reuse , extends fields,methods to another class(child)\n    - It represents \"is-a\" relationship.\n    -- > class EmailNotification(Notification):\n            def send(self, message):\n                print(f\"Sending email to {self.recipient}: {message}\")\na. Composition(param-class):\n    - Instead of inheriting behavior, simply pass the object into class as params\n    - creates loosser coupling bcz dependency can be replaced without changing the class hierarchy\n        --> class OrderService:\n            def __init__(self, email_sender):\n                self.email_sender = email_sender\n4. Polymorphism:\n    - same method name/interfaces to exhibit different behaviors depending on objects\n    - Compile-time Polymorphism(Method Overloading):\n        - methods with same name , but different params.\n        - python NOT support for add(int,int,int) -- use default - def add(self, *args): return sum(args)\n    - Runtime Polymorphism(Method Overriding):\n        - child class provides new implemenation for parent's method with same name,params\n            but different behavior.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "B85o9aWeteTGJmurhDz3W",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2163.0431694990725,
      "y": -364.4582921896833,
      "width": 689.4195556640625,
      "height": 125,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "d9Vfy9IuF5NXtY8wMEikm"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aC",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 48670274,
      "version": 279,
      "versionNonce": 648832962,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1787558156626,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Encapsulation = controls who can access or modify something\nAbstraction = controls which details the caller needs to see\nInheritance = one class is genuinely a specialized version of another\nComposition = one class simply needs to use another class's behavior\nPolymorphism = same methods name with different behavior's or params",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Encapsulation = controls who can access or modify something\nAbstraction = controls which details the caller needs to see\nInheritance = one class is genuinely a specialized version of another\nComposition = one class simply needs to use another class's behavior\nPolymorphism = same methods name with different behavior's or params",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
